+# ML3 reconciliation against the S-IDX ladder
+
+**STATUS: NEGATIVE for Stage 2; positive route identified.**
+
+## ⟨1⟩1. Decision
+
+**ASSUME.**  “Load-bearing for the Letter” means necessary for a statement
+that the Letter presents as a theorem, rather than an explicitly open
+conjecture or a possible proof route.
+
+**PROVE.**  **ML3 is not load-bearing for the Letter's narrow proved
+protocol theorem.**  The exact theorem surface available without ML3 is:
+
+> For the fully polarised bilinear ferromagnet `H_S`, `2S∈ℕ`, on the
+> primitive regular two-magnon channel and the separated-preparation class
+> `(D29-HS-SEP)`, every actual row-measure limit subsequence obeys
+> `A_*(ε)=i sgn(v_h-v_s)\bar k_*(ε)/S+O(ε²)` and has phase slope
+> `sgn(v_h-v_s)/S`.
+
+The Letter may place this protocol theorem beside the finite Ward index, but
+must not say that the latter proves the former.  The Ward-to-running-protocol
+bridge `(PROTO-LSZ)` remains open.  ML3 remains load-bearing only for the
+stronger packet-smeared current route specified in ⟨1⟩3 below, not for the
+displayed narrow theorem.
+
+*Justification.*  Claims `S-IDX-MATCH-HS-SEP`,
+`S-IDX-D29-value-HS-SEP`, `S2-2body-S`, and `S-IDX-fin-r2`.
+
+## ⟨1⟩2. Why the finite S-IDX theorem does not itself settle ML3
+
+**ASSUME.**  The full-sector and root-highest registers of the S-IDX ladder.
+
+**PROVE.**  The ladder separates a finite zero-mode identity from every
+running nonzero-soft-momentum statement.
+
+**⟨2⟩1.**  The finite theorem gives the descendant-range projection and
+normalized Ward index one; its pure zero-mode anchor explicitly makes no
+claim about the running protocol family.
+
+*Justification.*  Claims `S-IDX-fin-r2` and `S-IDX-fin-G`.
+
+**⟨2⟩2.**  General compact-group sector covariance gives a character-label
+difference, while the descendant projector is strictly finer than the
+target sector projector; labels therefore do not determine the projected
+current coefficient or a soft limit.
+
+*Justification.*  Claim `S-IDX-G-label`.
+
+**⟨2⟩3.**  Consequently S-IDX-fin cannot be cited as a proof that a physical
+`1/k_s` current pole is absent.  It supplies the finite Ward numerator, not
+the uniform limiting estimate.
+
+*Justification.*  Claims `S-IDX-fin-r2`, `S-IDX-fin-G`, and `ML3`.
+
+**⟨2⟩4. QED.**

